00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:52.590
And her character talks about that on Matlock, being useful.
她在《梅特洛克》中饰演的角色也谈到了这一点，即发挥作用。

156
00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:56.610
And that's something that I felt with my own journey.
这也是我在自己的人生旅程中感受到的。

157
00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:02.790
If I could just make this somehow useful, it keeps me going.
如果我能让这一切变得有意义，它就能支撑我继续前行。

158
00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.850
I really identified with that when she talked about that.
当她谈到这一点时，我真的很有共鸣。

159
00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:12.010
But guys, it was just an honor to have worked with her and to have her on the podcaSt.
但伙计们，能与她共事并邀请她来参加播客，真是我的荣幸。

160
00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.290
We're going to take a quick break.
我们要休息一下。

161
00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:17.050
And then our interview with Kathy Bates.
稍后带来我们对凯西·贝茨的采访。

162
00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:31.480
Hey, how are you?
嘿，你好吗？

163
00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:32.880
We're well, thank you.
我们很好，谢谢。

164
00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.660
Who's this puppy on your lap?
你腿上的这只小狗是谁？

165
00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:36.880
This is Zelda.
这是塞尔达。

166
00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:37.820
Hi, Zelda.
嗨，塞尔达。

167
00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.840
Yeah, she's in a licking mode.
是啊，她现在正处于爱舔人的状态。

168
00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:41.720
I don't know why.
我不知道为什么。

169
00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:43.700
Hi, how are you guys?
嗨，你们好吗？

170
00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.800
Oh, we're good.
哦，我们很好。

171
00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:50.140
It's so fun to get to see you again after all these years.
时隔多年又能见到你，真是太开心了。

172
00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:51.240
I know.
我知道。

173
00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.300
It's been, let's see, I think I did the show in 11, like 12 years ago.
算算看，我想我是在11年，也就是12年前参加的节目。

174
00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:57.580
Yeah.
是啊。

175
00:07:57.820 --> 00:07:57.960
Yeah.
是啊。

176
00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.880
12, 13 years ago.
12、13年前了。

177
00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.340
It's been a long time.
已经过去很久了。

178
00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:02.500
It's been a long time.
确实很久了。

179
00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.120
A lot of life has happened.
发生了很多人生变故。

180
00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:05.200
Yes, indeed.
确实如此。

181
00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:05.940
It has.
确实。

182
00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:07.680
How are you guys?
你们怎么样？

183
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:08.900
We're good.
我们很好。

184
00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.120
We're loving this new job of podcasting.
我们很喜欢这份做播客的新工作。

185
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:14.480
Kathy, I only got dressed on the top.
凯西，我只穿了上半身。

186
00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:15.780
Look at this.
你看。

187
00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.600
Just got my pajama pants on the bottom.
下面还穿着睡裤呢。

188
00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.720
I didn't even get dressed.
我甚至都没换衣服。

189
00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:24.840
I'm still in bed.
我还在床上呢。

190
00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.580
You are a master podcaster then.
那你已经是播客大师了。

191
00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:28.360
Already.
已经入门了。

192
00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.820
And we're basically in our closets here.
我们基本上也是在衣橱里录音。

193
00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:31.880
This is a closet.
这就是个衣橱。

194
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:33.400
I'm in a closet.
我就在衣橱里。

195
00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:34.300
Oh, I see.
哦，我明白了。

196
00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.980
That's how we do it.
我们就是这么干的。

197
00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.900
Kathy, thank you so much for joining us today.
凯西，非常感谢你今天能来参加我们的节目。

198
00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:47.380
We are so excited to talk with you about The Office, about your new show, Matlock.
我们非常兴奋能和你聊聊《办公室》，还有你的新剧《梅特洛克》。

199
00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.240
We are huge fans of Matlock.
我们是《梅特洛克》的超级粉丝。

200
00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.080
Thank you.
谢谢你们。

201
00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.580
Yes, we are loving it, Kathy.
是的，我们很喜欢，凯西。

202
00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:54.760
Yeah.
没错。

203
00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:59.600
And we also want to talk about your work with the Lymphatic Education and Research Network.
我们也想聊聊你与淋巴教育与研究网络合作的工作。

204
00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:00.720
Yes.
好的。

205
00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.480
Do you mind if we start with The Office?
你不介意我们从《办公室》开始聊吧？

206
00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:05.320
No, I don't mind at all.
不，我一点也不介意。

207
00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.160
Let's begin.
那我们开始吧。

208
00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:06.820
Okay.
好的。

209
00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.520
I'll kick us off with our first question.
我先来问第一个问题。

210
00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.020
And it's one we ask all of our guests that were on The Office.
这是我们问所有上过《办公室》节目的嘉宾的问题。

211
00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.600
And it's, how did you get your job on The Office?
那就是，你是怎么得到《办公室》这份工作的？

212
00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.880
I think they just offered it to me.
我想他们直接就邀请我了。

213
00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:19.960
I was very lucky.
我非常幸运。

214
00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.620
I had not seen the show that much, but I watched a great deal of it when I was preparing for the show.
我之前没怎么看过这部剧，但在准备拍摄时我看了很多。

215
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.180
And everyone was so brilliant and so effortless.
每个人都那么出色，表演得那么自然。

216
00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:31.420
You guys, I don't know how you did it.
你们大家，我不知道你们是怎么做到的。

217
00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:37.480
I felt like when I was doing it, I was really running to catch up and it was a learning curve.
我觉得我在拍的时候，真的在拼命追赶，那是一个学习的过程。

218
00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:48.580
I don't know if it was like that for you guys when you started working on the show, but working with the different cameras and the level and, you know,
不知道你们刚开始拍这部剧时是不是也这样，要配合不同的摄像机，还有那种水平，你知道的，

219
00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.640
the comedy that you guys did.
你们所展现的那种喜剧效果。

220
00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:57.200
I felt that I felt that I was really trying to get the feel of that and trying to do.
我感觉我一直在努力去把握那种感觉，努力去做好。

221
00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:04.160
I always felt after I did an episode that I wasn't sure if I'd really done it the best I could.
我总觉得每拍完一集，都不确定自己是否已经做到了最好。

222
00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.200
That's so interesting.
这太有意思了。

223
00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:11.200
I mean, our perspective was that you were nailing it.
我是说，我们的看法是你表现得非常完美。

224
00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.160
Like from the get-go.
就像从一开始就是那样。

225
00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:13.560
Yeah.
是吗。

226
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:18.320
Like when you walked in as Joe Bennett, I would have thought you'd been Joe Bennett your whole life.
就像你以乔·贝内特的身份走进来时，我会以为你这辈子就是乔·贝内特。

227
00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:19.220
Oh, really?
哦，真的吗？

228
00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:19.880
Yes.
是的。

229
00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:25.280
Yeah, it was such a fully realized character from the beginning.
没错，从一开始这就是一个非常完整的人物形象。

230
00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:26.080
Wow.
哇。

231
00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:36.140
Well, I think I was nervous because you guys do the comedy so well and I don't think of myself as a comedian and that you worked with the cameras so well.
嗯，我想我当时很紧张，因为你们把喜剧演得太好了，而我不认为自己是个喜剧演员，而且你们与摄像机的配合也那么默契。

232
00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:37.680
Was that a learning curve for you?
这对你来说是一个学习过程吗？

233
00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:43.980
I mean, did you, when you started out on it, did you, you had done, when did you start doing it?
我是说，当你刚开始拍的时候，你是什么时候开始接触的？

234
00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:44.880
I mean.
我是说。

235
00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:45.360
The camera?
摄像机吗？

236
00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:46.440
Yes.
是的。

237
00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.420
Well, the camera was part of my audition.
嗯，摄像机是我试镜的一部分。

238
00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.520
Yes, same.
是的，我也是。

239
00:10:50.860 --> 00:11:00.180
So from even the audition process, they were wanting me to relate to or not relate to the camera, throw looks to the camera.
所以从试镜开始，他们就要求我与摄像机互动或不互动，向摄像机投去目光。

240
00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:09.900
And I just studied the British version to prepare for my audition to watch because that was such a new idea.
为了准备试镜，我特意研究了英国版，因为那是一个非常新颖的概念。

241
00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:21.440
And also the idea, not just that you would relate to the camera, but that you would be embarrassed that the camera picked up on something that maybe you didn't want it to.
而且不仅仅是与摄像机互动，还有那种因为摄像机捕捉到了你可能不想让人看到的东西而感到尴尬的感觉。

242
00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:28.220
Kathy, I remember I was really nervous about how to interact with the camera in our pilot episode.
凯西，我记得在试播集里，我真的很紧张该如何与摄像机互动。

243
00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:33.200
You know, the supporting cast, we were just the background, you know, it was a real lesson in patience.
你知道，配角们，我们只是背景，那真是一堂关于耐心的课。

244
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:38.720
And you just had to wait for that moment, you know, when you finally got to have a little interaction.
你必须等待那个时刻，你知道的，当你终于有机会进行一点互动的时候。

245
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:44.120
But we spent so much time in the background and my nose was itching so bad one day.
但我们花了很多时间在背景里，有一天我的鼻子痒得厉害。

246
00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.900
And I thought, oh, I can't scratch it because I'm on camera.
我想，噢，我不能抓，因为我在镜头里。

247
00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:48.580
That's weird.
那太奇怪了。

248
00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:51.440
And I was, oh, God, I wanted to scratch it so bad.
我当时想，天哪，我太想抓它了。

249
00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:56.640
And I went up to Ken Quapas, our director, and he must have thought I was like so silly.
我去找了我们的导演肯·夸帕斯，他一定觉得我太傻了。

250
00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:03.060
I said, Ken, my nose was really itching in that last take and I scratched it and I'm so sorry.
我说，肯，上一条里我的鼻子真的很痒，我抓了它，真的很抱歉。

251
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.340
And he was like, Angela, you are living a life.
他当时就说，安吉拉，你活出了自我。

252
00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:07.200
Live a life.
活出自我。

253
00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:08.920
And the camera captures what it captures.
镜头拍到什么就是什么。

254
00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:10.580
If your nose itches, scratch it.
鼻子痒了，就抓一抓。

255
00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:16.060
And that was sort of an eye-opening moment for me about how to just be in the background.
那对我来说算是一个启发性的时刻，让我明白了如何自然地作为背景存在。

256
00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:16.780
Yeah.
是啊。

257
00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:23.100
One of the episodes that I think I wish I had prepared for first was when Joe comes in and
有一集我真希望自己能提前做好准备，就是乔进来然后

258
00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.540
starts talking about the printers.
开始谈论打印机的那一集。

259
00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:29.800
You know, I would like to have gone back and done that and really gone to town with it.
你知道，我真想回到过去重拍那场戏，好好发挥一下。

260
00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:33.720
But the other ones I really loved working with.
但其他的戏份我真的很喜欢参与。

261
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:39.820
I mean, Carell, Steve Carell, I couldn't get over what a fantastic actor.
我是说，卡瑞尔，史蒂夫·卡瑞尔，他真是位了不起的演员，我对他佩服得五体投地。

262
00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:42.760
And improvisationalist he is.
而且他即兴表演的能力太强了。

263
00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:50.220
So for me, it was great to be around people like him and John and a whole bunch of the
所以对我来说，能和像他、约翰以及其他一大群

264
00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:51.120
guys and gals.
男男女女共事真是太棒了。

265
00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:58.380
It's just to try and learn from them how they do what they do because it's invisible.
我只是想试着向他们学习，看他们是如何做到那些表演的，因为那看起来是无形的。

266
00:12:58.920 --> 00:12:59.860
Does that make sense?
这说得通吗？

267
00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:00.480
Yeah.
是啊。

268
00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:01.460
Yeah, it does.
是的，说得通。

269
00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:04.720
You don't feel like you're watching someone act when you watch Steve.
你看史蒂夫演戏时，不会觉得他在演戏。

270
00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:05.740
Like I never do.
反正我从来没这种感觉。

271
00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:06.940
I always believed him.
我总是相信他塑造的角色。

272
00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:11.760
Um, he just is such a natural in whatever role he puts on.
嗯，无论他扮演什么角色，他都表现得如此自然。

273
00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.140
I don't see him acting.
我看不出他在演戏。

274
00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:14.920
I didn't see him as DuPont.
我当时都没认出那是他演的杜邦。

275
00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.680
Oh, in Foxcatcher.
哦，在《狐狸猎手》里。

276
00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:17.640
That's right.
没错。

277
00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.960
He was nominated for an Academy Award.
他当时还获得了奥斯卡奖提名。

278
00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:20.660
Yes.
是的。

279
00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:21.040
Yeah.
是啊。

280
00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:21.380
Yes.
没错。

281
00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:28.340
I was watching it with a friend and we were in about 15 minutes and he said, you know who
我当时和朋友一起看，看了大约15分钟，他说，你知道那是

282
00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.000
that is?
谁吗？

283
00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.320
I said, no, who is that?
我说，不知道，那是谁？

284
00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:33.080
He said, that's Steve Carell.
他说，那是史蒂夫·卡瑞尔。

285
00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.000
I said, you've got to be kidding me.
我说，你一定是在开玩笑。

286
00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.760
I can't believe that Steve Carell.
我简直不敢相信那是史蒂夫·卡瑞尔。

287
00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:39.740
I mean, he was just, he disappeared into the character.
我是说，他完全融入了角色之中。

288
00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:45.960
But back on, on working with him at the office, I just wish I could have learned more from
但回到在《办公室》和他共事的话题，我真希望当时能从他身上学到更多。

289
00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:46.300
him.
东西。

290
00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.560
You know, I certainly enjoyed working on the show.
你知道，我确实很享受在那部剧里的工作。

291
00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.060
I thought it was a lot of fun.
我觉得那非常有趣。

292
00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:57.900
I did have a problem transitioning from lines that were written and then the improvisation
在处理剧本台词和我们被允许进行的即兴表演之间的转换时，我确实遇到过困难。

293
00:13:57.900 --> 00:13:59.100
that we were allowed to do.
我不知道自己是否真的成功过。

294
00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:01.440
I don't know if I ever really succeeded.
我总是被这一点绊住，但那确实是一段美妙的经历。

295
00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:06.340
I kept getting tripped up on that, but it was a wonderful experience.
我知道在剧集快结束时，因为史蒂夫要离开，他们曾有意让我留下来，

296
00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:11.840
I know toward the end, since Steve was leaving, they were interested in me possibly staying
但我当时已经去拍《律政俏师太》了。

297
00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:15.420
on and I had gone on to do Harry's Law.
不过，你知道，我不想诋毁任何人，但我觉得如果能留在《办公室》应该会很有趣，

298
00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:23.740
But, you know, I don't want to cast aspersions on anybody, but I think it would have been fun
你知道的。

299
00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:25.540
to stay on the office, you know.
我们当时会很乐意那样做的。

300
00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.300
We would have loved that.
我们当时会很乐意那样做的。

301
00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:30.700
Yeah, for sure.
没错，确实如此。

302
00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:32.180
For sure.
确实。

303
00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.360
And really developed that character more, you know.
而且真的把那个角色塑造得更丰满了，你知道的。

304
00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:38.240
She had so much, you know, she was so layered.
她有很多，你知道，她非常有层次感。

305
00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:43.140
I mean, the scenes between Michael and Joe on the private plane were just so brilliant.
我是说，迈克尔和乔在私人飞机上的那些戏份真是太精彩了。

306
00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:43.940
I loved those.
我超爱那些戏。

307
00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:45.640
I wanted to see more of her world.
我当时想看到更多关于她的世界。

308
00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:46.900
Like, where does she go?
比如，她会去哪里？

309
00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.520
And the Great Danes and your outfits and your hair.
还有那些大丹犬，以及你的装扮和发型。

310
00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.680
Were you a part of the look of Joe Bennett?
你参与了乔·贝内特的造型设计吗？

311
00:14:52.680 --> 00:15:00.060
Yeah, I had this fur coat that I had found that was kind of strips of fur that was kind
是的，我当时找了一件皮草大衣，那种皮草条纹的，看起来

312
00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.200
of really kind of low class.
真的很廉价。

313
00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.340
But I insisted on wearing that.
但我坚持要穿那件。

314
00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:07.040
That was just so inappropriate.
那真的太不合时宜了。

315
00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:13.320
And I think, yes, we worked on the hair and the look for her.
我想是的，我们确实为她的发型和造型下了一番功夫。

316
00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:14.760
And it's been a while.
而且那已经是好久以前的事了。

317
00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:15.500
So forgive me.
所以请原谅我。

318
00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:20.920
I can't remember the wonderful people who worked with me on that to really create something
我不记得当时和我一起合作创造出那种

319
00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:24.180
that was out of style, you know.
过时风格的那些出色的人员了，你知道的。

320
00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:28.500
I think she figured it's what they say when you figure out what style looks good on you.
我想她认为，这就是人们所说的，当你找到适合自己的风格时。

321
00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:33.040
You stay with it, regardless of your age or anything or fashion.
无论年龄或时尚潮流如何，你都会坚持下去。

322
00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:35.720
And also just her swagger.
还有她的那种气场。

323
00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:41.280
You know, I felt she had a lot of swagger and that she was probably a bull in a China
你知道，我觉得她很有气场，而且她可能就像闯进瓷器店的

324
00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:41.700
shop.
公牛一样。

325
00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:50.100
That's how she ran her company was just sort of steamrolling over everybody and with her
她经营公司的方式就是用她的钱

326
00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:50.480
money.
横冲直撞，碾压所有人。

327
00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:53.660
And I loved the Great Danes.
而且我超爱那些大丹犬。

328
00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:54.540
I really did.
我真的很喜欢。

329
00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:55.180
They were great.
它们太棒了。

330
00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:57.700
There was one scene where I said, let's have the Great Dane.
有一场戏我说，我们要让大丹犬出镜。

331
00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:00.800
We have to have one of them staring over my shoulder.
我们得让其中一只盯着我的肩膀看。

332
00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.520
Yes, I remember that.
是的，我记得那场戏。

333
00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:03.680
It's so brilliant.
太精彩了。

334
00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:10.840
Yeah, the trainers walk around with a lot of baby diapers, the old fashioned ones, because
是啊，驯兽师随身带着很多婴儿尿布，那种老式的，因为

335
00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:12.600
those dogs slobber a lot.
那些狗流很多口水。

336
00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.620
So would they wipe them with the baby diaper?
所以他们会用婴儿尿布给它们擦吗？

337
00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.720
Yeah, the baby, they keep their slobber there from coming out.
对，他们用那个来接住它们的口水，防止流出来。

338
00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:23.080
And they were a mother and a son, actually.
而且它们其实是一对母子。

339
00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:28.360
And I think they weighed about 120 pounds each, just huge, but really sweet.
我想它们每只大概有120磅重，简直巨大，但真的很温顺。

340
00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:32.340
You know, not really, you know, not really bright.
你知道，其实不太，你知道，不太聪明。

341
00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:36.600
But they were, you know, we had fun with them.
但它们，你知道，我们和它们相处得很开心。

342
00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:39.480
And I loved the scene where one was looking over my shoulder.
我特别喜欢其中一只盯着我肩膀看的那场戏。

343
00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:43.840
So when that one looks over your shoulder, its head is bigger than yours.
所以当那只狗盯着你肩膀看的时候，它的头比你的还大。

344
00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:47.000
It's like its presence on camera is so big.
它在镜头里的存在感简直太强了。

345
00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:49.840
Yeah, we kept trying to get him to bark.
是啊，我们一直试图让它叫。

346
00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:51.280
I don't know if we ever succeeded.
我不知道我们最后成功了没有。

347
00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:52.440
I can't remember now.
我现在记不清了。

348
00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:56.280
What was it like for you to be reunited with Rainn Wilson?
再次与雷恩·威尔森合作感觉如何？

349
00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:00.780
He shared with us that you gave him his big break on Six Feet Under.
他跟我们分享说，是你让他通过《六尺之下》获得了成名的机会。

350
00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:01.680
You know what?
你知道吗？

351
00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:02.500
He was so funny.
他太搞笑了。

352
00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:03.960
I'll always have this memory.
我会永远记得这段回忆。

353
00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:08.240
He came up for some reason we were shooting in Ruth's room.
当时我们在露丝的房间拍摄，他不知怎么就过来了。

354
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.800
And he had just been hired at the funeral home.
他刚被殡仪馆录用。

355
00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:15.380
And so he was rushing out the door.
所以他正急着出门。

356
00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:16.420
He had to do something.
他得去办点事。

357
00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.020
We gave him something to do.
我们给他安排了点活儿。

358
00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:19.480
We had to go do it really quickly.
我们得赶紧去把它做完。

359
00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:23.220
And he ran out into the hallway and he took a right.
他跑进走廊，然后向右转。

360
00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:25.680
And then there was this pause.
接着停顿了一下。

361
00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:33.100
And he ran left because he realized that he didn't know where he was going because it was a completely new environment for him.
然后他又向左跑，因为他意识到自己不知道该往哪走，毕竟对他来说这是一个全新的环境。

362
00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:35.740
And I thought, oh, my God, this guy is brilliant.
我当时心想，天哪，这家伙真是个天才。

363
00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.120
It was such an amazing choice.
这个选择太棒了。

364
00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:39.180
It was a shock.
这让人很震惊。

365
00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:41.460
He was so wonderful to work with.
和他合作真是太美妙了。

366
00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:42.460
So inventive.
非常有创意。

367
00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:43.180
So funny.
非常幽默。

368
00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:47.400
Well, all of the things that you saw working with him on the show, I'm sure.
我想，你们在节目中和他合作时看到的那些特质，他都有。

369
00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:51.840
When I worked with him on your show, he had evolved quite a bit, you know, as an actor.
当我和他在你们的节目中合作时，作为一名演员，他已经有了很大的进步。

370
00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.140
And he's a very funny guy.
而且他是个非常风趣的人。

371
00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:55.960
Really, really liked working with him.
真的，我非常喜欢和他共事。

372
00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.440
Well, you know, we are both watching Matlock and we are loving it.
嗯，你知道，我们俩都在看《马特洛克》，而且我们都非常喜欢。

373
00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:01.760
I mean, everyone is loving it.
我是说，每个人都喜欢它。

374
00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.800
Kathy, it is just taken off like wildfire.
凯西，它简直火得一塌糊涂。

375
00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:07.660
And we would love to talk to you about it.
我们很想和你聊聊这部剧。

376
00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:09.760
Well, I'm happy to talk about it.
好啊，我很乐意聊聊。

377
00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.760
Yeah, we're where the numbers are just crazy.
是啊，收视数据简直疯狂。

378
00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:14.860
They were just off the charts.
简直高得离谱。

379
00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:26.060
And the best part about it is that everybody involved with it, whether it's the president of the studio, Amy Reisenbach, who's been our big fan.
最棒的部分在于所有参与其中的人，无论是工作室总裁艾米·雷森巴赫，她一直是我们的忠实粉丝。

380
00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:31.900
And David Staff, the president of the studio, and George Sheeks, who's kind of over all of us in Paramount.
还有工作室总裁大卫·斯塔夫，以及派拉蒙负责统筹我们的乔治·希克斯。

381
00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:37.460
They're all so normal, you know, and they're accessible.
他们都非常平易近人，你知道，很容易沟通。

382
00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:43.560
They communicate and they send emails and, you know, they came to our last table read.
他们会交流，会发邮件，而且，你知道，他们还参加了我们上一次的剧本围读。

383
00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:45.280
And I've never had that happen before.
我以前从未遇到过这种情况。

384
00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:49.500
And then, of course, there's Jenny Ehrman, who created Jane the Virgin.
当然，还有创作了《处女情缘》的珍妮·厄曼。

385
00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:52.240
And she's brilliant.
她是个天才。

386
00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:54.920
Her mind works like a Rubik's Cube.
她的思维就像魔方一样灵活。

387
00:18:54.920 --> 00:19:02.460
And the layers that she's created in this series is just fascinating.
她在本剧中构建的层次感简直令人着迷。

388
00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:05.920
And I'm also an executive producer on the show.
我也是这部剧的执行制片人。

389
00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:09.920
So they send me the episodes to take a look at, see if I have any suggestions.
所以他们会把剧集发给我看，看看我有没有什么建议。

390
00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:14.500
And I mean, it's just I can't wait.
我是说，我简直等不及了。

391
00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:15.600
I can't wait.
我等不及了。

392
00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.680
I can't wait for what's coming up.
我迫不及待想看接下来的剧情了。

393
00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:28.180
So when I first met Jenny, you know, I first read the script and as I was reading along, it seemed like it was just episodic, you know, that it was a case of the week.
所以当我第一次见到珍妮时，你知道，我刚读完剧本，读的时候觉得这只是单元剧，你知道，就是每周一个案子那种。

394
00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:28.880
Yes.
是的。

395
00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.960
And then, of course, there's the twist at the end.
然后，当然，结尾有个反转。

396
00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:34.740
And I was thrilled because I think, OK, now this is about substance.
我当时激动坏了，因为我想，好吧，这下有深度了。

397
00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:36.620
It's about something.
这剧有内涵。

398
00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:37.980
It's about something meaningful.
它探讨了一些有意义的东西。

399
00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:40.660
It's something I can really sink my teeth into.
这是我真正可以大展拳脚的角色。

400
00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:43.500
And I read it on a Friday.
我是周五读到剧本的。

401
00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:46.320
I met with her on a Monday and I said, don't change a word.
我周一和她见了面，我说，一个字都别改。

402
00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:47.540
I want to do this.
我想演这个角色。

403
00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:58.180
And then we started talking about the character and where it was going and the the who she was, where she came from, all of the things,
然后我们开始讨论这个角色，讨论她的走向，她是谁，她从哪里来，所有这些细节，

404
00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:05.680
how she had planned this all out with her grandson, you know, and it was it was fascinating.
她是如何与孙子一起筹划这一切的，你知道，这真的太迷人了。

405
00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:11.400
I just I didn't I've never imagined playing a character like this.
我只是……我从未想过自己会扮演这样的角色。

406
00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:14.900
Well, you're really playing two characters, right?
嗯，你实际上是在演两个角色，对吧？

407
00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:15.000
Right.
没错。

408
00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:18.720
So you're playing this sort of like.
所以你演的是这种……

409
00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:27.120
Like, explain that to people, because if you watch the first episode, if you watch the pilot, it's one of the best pilots of anything I've ever seen.
给大家解释一下吧，因为如果你看了第一集，也就是试播集，那是我看过最棒的试播集之一。

410
00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:32.200
I remember seeing the pilot for Breaking Bad and thinking, wow, that was a great pilot.
我记得当年看《绝命毒师》的试播集时，心想，哇，这集拍得真好。

411
00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:35.500
And I felt the same way when I saw the pilot for Matlock.
看《女律师马特洛克》的试播集时，我也有同样的感觉。

412
00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:36.100
Wow.
哇。

413
00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:38.320
The twist at the end is genius.
结尾的反转简直是天才之作。

414
00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:42.320
But you had me from the minute you stood at that coffee counter.
但从你站在咖啡柜台前的那一刻起，你就已经抓住我了。

415
00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:42.900
Yes.
是的。

416
00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.120
And you're bumbling.
你表现得笨手笨脚的。

417
00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:46.080
You get a guy to pay for your coffee.
你让别人帮你付咖啡钱。

418
00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:47.380
You get the candies.
你拿了糖果。

419
00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:49.420
You put your candies in your hand.
你把糖果抓在手里。

420
00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:58.840
And you trick your way in to this law firm into their boardroom into a job.
然后你用计混进了那家律师事务所，进了他们的董事会，还得到了一份工作。

421
00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.280
It is amazing.
这太神奇了。

422
00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:01.660
I think so, too.
我也这么觉得。

423
00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:07.420
I mean, I had to keep asking Jenny questions about, is this possible?
我是说，我不得不一直问珍妮问题，比如，这真的可能吗？

424
00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:13.240
Well, I remember asking a friend of mine who's in corporate law, not corporate law, but he's in the corporate world.
嗯，我记得问过我的一位朋友，他在企业法领域工作，不，不是企业法，但他身处企业界。

425
00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:15.600
He's a dear friend of mine.
他是我的一位挚友。

426
00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:17.780
I said, could you do this?
我说，你能做到这样吗？

427
00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:19.760
Could you do this?
你能做到吗？

428
00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:21.660
And he thought about it for a minute.
他思考了一会儿。

429
00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.480
He said, yeah, I could.
他说，能，我能做到。

430
00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:26.240
And then I don't have kids.
而且我没有孩子。

431
00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:27.280
So I talked to my niece.
所以我问了我的侄女。

432
00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:28.780
She has a daughter who's now grown.
她有个女儿，现在已经长大了。

433
00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:31.900
And I said, but could you do this?
我说，你能做到这样吗？

434
00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:39.080
Could you be this, you know, devious and deceitful and lie to people all the time for your daughter?
为了你的女儿，你能变得如此狡猾、虚伪，并一直对人撒谎吗？

435
00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:40.800
And she said, yes.
她说，能。

436
00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:46.060
She said, when you're a mother, your love for your child is savage.
她说，当你成为母亲，你对孩子的爱是野蛮而强烈的。

437
00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:51.240
And I love, of course, the fact that she's older.
当然，我非常喜欢她年纪较大这一点。

438
00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:56.600
I would never have believed that I could have done a series like this at my age, been asked to do one.
我从没想过自己在这个年纪还能演这样的剧集，还能受邀出演。

439
00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:01.460
And that we've gotten letters from people, from women who say, thank you.
我们收到了很多人的来信，来自那些女性，她们说，谢谢你们。

440
00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:03.180
I don't feel invisible anymore.
我不再觉得自己是个隐形人了。

441
00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:12.680
I think that you'll see as the series goes on that Matlock is really falling in love with being a lawyer again.
我想随着剧集的发展，你会看到马特洛克真的重新爱上了当律师的感觉。

442
00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:16.060
You know, she was in mourning for years and years.
你知道，她哀悼了整整好多年。

443
00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:22.720
And then her grandson saw this Reddit post and said, you know, this is a way to get back at them.
然后她的孙子看到了那篇Reddit帖子，说，你知道，这是报复他们的好办法。

444
00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.860
You know, she can plan everything she wants.
你知道，她可以筹划她想要的一切。

445
00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:30.060
But she doesn't expect to be a trial lawyer.
但她没料到自己会成为一名出庭律师。

446
00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:39.600
And you find out later on in the third episode that she, you know, went into a contract law because of a sexual abuse experience.
后来在第三集你会发现，她是因为曾经遭受过性虐待，才选择了合同法。

447
00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:43.420
And instead of going into litigation, which is what she really wanted.
而不是去从事她真正想做的诉讼工作。

448
00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:46.960
And I'm jumping all over here.
我有点跳跃了。

449
00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:58.640
But that was the seminal moment for her, that she was really able to make a difference, that she was able to remember her own sexual assault.
但那是她人生中决定性的时刻，她终于能够有所作为，能够正视自己曾遭受的性侵经历。

450
00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:04.220
And it changed her opinion about what young women go through nowadays.
这改变了她对当今年轻女性所经历之事的看法。

451
00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:17.080
And it also shows not just the sexual assault, which is what we all talk about and how bad that is and how prevalent that is.
它不仅展现了性侵犯——这是我们都在讨论的话题，也谈到了它的恶劣程度和普遍性。

452
00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:22.980
But it talks about what happens then, what happens after.
它还探讨了在那之后会发生什么，后续的影响。

453
00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:31.240
Do you make a different career choice for women that are trying to get into show business and they get into a really bad situation?
对于那些试图进入演艺圈却陷入极其糟糕境地的女性，你会为她们做出不同的职业选择吗？

454
00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.140
What happens to them after that?
在那之后她们会经历什么？

455
00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:39.760
And in this case, we see Maddie taking a divergent path, one that she wasn't happy with.
在这个案例中，我们看到麦迪选择了一条不同的道路，一条她并不快乐的道路。

456
00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:43.340
And she didn't realize that until she met this young woman.
直到她遇见这个年轻女孩，她才意识到这一点。

457
00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:53.440
So the fact that she's learning all of this from everyone is a very powerful thing to say in an episodic television show on network.
所以，她在每个人身上学到这一切，这在电视网播出的剧集中是一个非常有力的表达。

458
00:23:53.440 --> 00:24:03.000
I was going to say the idea, you know, I'm 53, but I'm still discovering things about myself and that life is a discovery.
我正想说这个想法，你知道，我53岁了，但我仍在探索自我，生活本身就是一场探索。

459
00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:04.500
You don't age out of discovery.
你不会因为年龄增长而停止探索。

460
00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.100
And I think sometimes we dismiss that.
我觉得有时我们忽略了这一点。

461
00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:10.220
We think that we can't discover new things about ourselves.
我们认为自己无法再发现关于自我的新东西。

462
00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:12.500
And I've really connected to that in this show.
而我在这个节目中真正体会到了这一点。

463
00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:14.060
Yeah, I agree.
是的，我同意。

464
00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:16.300
That's a really good point.
这是一个非常好的观点。

465
00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.440
Every time I do something, I want to learn from it.
每次我做某件事，我都想从中学习。

466
00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:30.260
And one of the most valuable things, as I was saying, being an exec producer and being able to see the cuts, I'm learning things about my acting, like bad habits, you know,
正如我所说，最宝贵的事情之一就是作为执行制片人能够看到剪辑片段，我正在学习关于我表演的东西，比如坏习惯，你知道，

467
00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.320
that I want to change without restricting myself.
我想在不限制自己的前提下做出改变。

468
00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:35.660
But we're always learning.
但我们总是在学习。

469
00:24:35.860 --> 00:24:38.920
We're always evolving as artists, as people.
作为艺术家，作为个人，我们总是在进化。

470
00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:40.860
We're trying to learn from our experiences.
我们正试图从经验中学习。

471
00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:50.280
And seeing that happen in a show like this is so, I think that's one of the reasons people are so attracted to it.
看到这一切在这样的节目中发生，我想这就是人们如此被它吸引的原因之一。

472
00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:55.500
And it's people from all walks of life, which is what I really like.
而且这是来自各行各业的人，这正是我真正喜欢的。

473
00:24:56.020 --> 00:25:06.100
I feel so grateful that everyone on the show, I think that even the crew, there was a sense when we came back to do episode two, because it was the first time we'd shot in America.
我感到非常感激，节目组的每个人，我想甚至包括剧组工作人员，当我们回来拍摄第二集时，有一种特别的感觉，因为这是我们第一次在美国拍摄。

474
00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:07.620
We shot the pilot in Toronto.
我们在多伦多拍摄了试播集。

475
00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:15.700
So when we came back to do it here, there was just this buzz and this excitement about getting ready to do the show.
所以当我们回来这里拍摄时，那种为准备节目而产生的热烈氛围和兴奋感油然而生。

476
00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:20.560
And the crew, you could feel it on the set.
剧组工作人员，你在片场就能感受到。

477
00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:26.320
It was just, it was, it was new to me to feel that.
这对我来说，这是一种全新的感受。

478
00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:37.480
And so, I mean, we would all go around looking at each other or even the day players or the guest cast that would come in would just be looking at us going, oh my God.
所以，我的意思是，我们都会环顾四周看着彼此，甚至连临时演员或客串演员进来时，都会看着我们说，天哪。

479
00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:39.820
We can't believe it's like this.
我们简直不敢相信会是这样。

480
00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:42.520
We've never worked on a show like this.
我们从未参与过这样的节目。

481
00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:48.480
So it's not just the material that we're putting out for everybody.
所以这不仅仅是我们呈现给观众的内容。

482
00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:59.760
It's also the experience of doing it every day and the joy of connecting and being vulnerable to one another so that the work really blossoMs.
这也是每天参与其中的体验，以及那种相互连接、彼此坦诚所带来的快乐，让作品真正绽放光彩。

483
00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:05.000
And if you learn something about yourself, all I can say is I'm grateful.
如果你能从中了解自己，我只能说我很感激。

484
00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.920
I'm just so grateful to have this experience.
我非常感激能有这次经历。

485
00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:15.400
Well, the way you talk about it and the way your face lights up and the way you talk about the people and the crew and the experience,
嗯，你谈论它的方式，你脸上洋溢的神采，以及你谈论演员、剧组和这段经历的方式，

486
00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:18.620
it just reminds me so much of working on The Office.
这让我想起了在《办公室》工作时的情景。

487
00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:19.140
Yes.
是的。

488
00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:19.980
Doesn't it you, Angela?
你不觉得吗，安吉拉？

489
00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:26.440
Yeah, so much, especially, Kathy, when you were talking about how exciting it is to get the scripts and have an input.
是的，太像了，尤其是凯西，当你谈到拿到剧本并能提出意见是多么令人兴奋时。

490
00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.360
You know, there was such a creative collaboration.
你知道，那是一种非常棒的创意协作。

491
00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:34.000
Greg Daniels set the tone for that, that we could go to the writers and we could pitch ideas to them.
格雷格·丹尼尔斯定下了基调，我们可以去找编剧，向他们提出想法。

492
00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:38.520
And that was so powerful and meaningful even as a supporting cast member.
即使作为配角，那也是非常有力量和意义的。

493
00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:40.020
So I love that.
所以我很喜欢这一点。

494
00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:41.440
And you see it in the work.
你可以在作品中看到这一点。

495
00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:43.420
You see the creative collaboration.
你可以看到那种创意协作。

496
00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:46.020
It's why we're hooked from the minute you're on screen.
这就是为什么我们从你出现在屏幕上的那一刻起就被深深吸引。

497
00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:48.360
The cast is so fantastic.
演员阵容太棒了。

498
00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:50.680
I really love the caSt.
我真的很喜欢这个演员阵容。

499
00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:52.320
And we can't wait.
我们已经迫不及待了。

500
00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:54.300
You know, we'll keep talking about it.
你知道，我们会继续讨论它的。

501
00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:58.440
Well, I felt like for a minute, I felt you were talking about The Office.
嗯，有一瞬间我感觉你是在谈论《办公室》这部剧。

502
00:26:58.640 --> 00:26:59.100
Oh.
哦。

503
00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:00.920
You know, because it's the same way.
你知道，因为感觉是一样的。

504
00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:04.060
I mean, you guys, you were full-blown people.
我是说，你们当时就是活生生的人。

505
00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:08.640
And there was, as you said before, you're living.
正如你之前所说，你们是在真实地生活。

506
00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:11.500
That's what I'm always saying to Jenny.
我总是对珍妮这么说。

507
00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:13.060
I don't want to play Maddie.
我不想扮演麦迪。

508
00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:14.140
I want to be Maddie.
我想成为麦迪。

509
00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:17.520
So that's what you guys were doing.
所以你们当时就是这么做的。

510
00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:23.720
And the feeling on the set of The Office is, I thought you were talking about you guys.
而《办公室》片场的那种感觉，我原以为你在说你们剧组。

511
00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:24.840
It's the same thing.
是一样的。

512
00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:25.480
It is.
确实。

513
00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:26.580
It's the same thing.
是一样的。

514
00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:43.240
Well, you know, we also would love to talk to you about your advocacy work
嗯，你知道，我们也想和你聊聊你的倡导工作，

515
00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:45.220
and your recent health journey.
以及你最近的健康历程。

516
00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:48.480
And I know Jenna had reached out to you.
我知道珍娜联系过你。

517
00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:52.140
And I would love to start that part of this conversation.
我很想从这部分开始我们的谈话。

518
00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:52.840
Right.
好的。

519
00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:56.960
Jenna, can you tell me where you are in terms of your...
珍娜，你能告诉我你现在的状况吗……

520
00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:59.280
I know I heard that you have breast cancer.
我知道我听说你患了乳腺癌。

521
00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:02.380
Could you tell me a little bit about where you are in that journey?
你能跟我说说你现在的治疗进展吗？

522
00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:03.300
Sure.
当然。

523
00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:07.240
So I was diagnosed last December, December 2023.
我是去年十二月，也就是2023年十二月被确诊的。

524
00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.820
In January, I had a lumpectomy.
一月份，我做了肿块切除手术。

525
00:28:11.020 --> 00:28:14.660
And then I had a reconstructive surgery.
然后我做了重建手术。

526
00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:17.660
I had a breast reduction and lift.
我做了缩胸和提拉手术。

527
00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:22.520
And what they were able to do was take some of my own breast tissue
他们能够利用我自身的一些乳房组织，

528
00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:26.120
and sort of fill in the hole where they removed the cancer.
填补切除癌症后留下的空洞。

529
00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:31.020
So I was reconstructed on the one side using my own tissue.
所以我的一侧是用我自己的组织重建的。

530
00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:33.080
And then they made the other side match.
然后他们让另一侧与之匹配。

531
00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:41.060
And then I did 12 rounds of taxol chemotherapy.
接着我进行了12轮紫杉醇化疗。

532
00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:42.900
I got that once a week.
我每周做一次。

533
00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:45.880
And I did three weeks of radiation.
我还做了三周的放疗。

534
00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:50.060
And my breast cancer is triple positive.
我的乳腺癌是三阳性的。

535
00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:53.920
So it was positive for estrogen, progesterone, and HER2.
也就是说雌激素、孕激素和HER2受体均为阳性。

536
00:28:55.380 --> 00:29:02.300
And so for a year, I received these immunotherapy infusions
所以一年来，我一直在接受一种名为赫赛汀的免疫疗法输液，

537
00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:06.120
of a drug called Herceptin, which is a miracle drug
这是一种奇迹般的药物，

538
00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:08.500
that was discovered in my lifetime.
是在我有生之年被发现的。

539
00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:13.940
Before this drug was introduced to the treatment plan
在这种药物被引入治疗方案之前，

540
00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:16.220
for women with HER2-positive breast cancers,
对于HER2阳性乳腺癌女性患者来说，

541
00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:20.100
the survival rate was one of the lowest
其存活率是所有乳腺癌诊断中，

542
00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.420
of the breast cancer diagnoses.
最低的之一。

543
00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:24.480
And now it's one of the higheSt.
而现在它是最高的之一。

544
00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:29.600
And so I feel very blessed that this is part of my treatment plan.
所以我感到非常幸运，这是我治疗方案的一部分。

545
00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:34.060
And so I've got a few more infusions of that ahead of me.
我还有几次输液要进行。

546
00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:35.920
I get those once every three weeks.
我每三周做一次。

547
00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:42.520
And then I will be on tamoxifen, which is an estrogen blocker,
之后我将服用他莫昔芬，这是一种雌激素阻断剂，

548
00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:44.000
for five years.
要服用五年。

549
00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.560
And that's my treatment plan.
这就是我的治疗方案。

550
00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:50.020
So I'm finished with the big three, as I called them,
所以我已经完成了我所说的“三大难关”，

551
00:29:50.020 --> 00:29:52.640
the surgery, the chemo, and the radiation.
手术、化疗，还有放疗。

552
00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:57.660
And now I still have a few things ahead of me.
而现在我还有一些后续治疗要做。

553
00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:03.460
But I'm riding waves of random side effects every day.
但我每天都在经历各种随机出现的副作用。

554
00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:04.860
It's a little different.
情况有点不同。

555
00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:12.400
You know, it's, I know you, I don't know if you can relate,
你知道，我不知道你能不能感同身受，

556
00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:15.940
but one of the hardest parts of this whole process has been
但整个过程中最难的部分之一，

557
00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:21.660
just the intense need for flexibility on a day-to-day basis,
就是每天都需要极强的灵活性，

558
00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:25.260
according to what my body can do, you know?
要根据我身体的状况来调整，你懂吗？

559
00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:29.200
And that's hard for me because I'm a real planner.
这对我很困难，因为我是一个凡事都要做计划的人。

560
00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:30.360
I love a schedule.
我喜欢按日程表行事。

561
00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:33.600
I love to do what I say I was going to do.
我喜欢说到做到。

562
00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:38.100
And so that's kind of where I am in the process.
这就是我目前治疗进程的情况。

563
00:30:38.420 --> 00:30:42.920
Well, I had breast cancer, let's see, right,
嗯，我得过乳腺癌，让我想想，对，

564
00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:46.920
I think right as I started, after I started working with you guys,
我想就在我开始，在我和你们共事之后，

565
00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:47.640
it was 2012.
那是2012年。

566
00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:48.920
Right after, yeah.
就在那之后，没错。

567
00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:55.780
And I had a very bad experience with it.
那次经历非常糟糕。

568
00:30:57.260 --> 00:31:01.000
I'd had ovarian in 2003.
我在2003年得过卵巢癌。

569
00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:05.720
And for some reason, even though I went through nine rounds of chemo for that,
不知为何，尽管当时我接受了九轮化疗，

570
00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.180
that was, the chemo was really hard.
那次化疗真的非常痛苦。

571
00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:15.300
It was, we had to do, I can't remember the names of the drugs,
当时我们必须做，我不记得药物的名字了，

572
00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:20.660
but I remember they gave me steroids and then I would be coming off the steroids
但我记得他们给我用了类固醇，然后等药效过去，

573
00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:23.360
and just getting, I don't know if you've gone through that,
那种感觉，不知道你有没有经历过，

574
00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:24.720
but that's been really difficult.
真的非常难受。

575
00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:26.240
That was very difficult.
那段日子非常煎熬。

576
00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:30.220
I got steroids before each round of chemo.
我在每一轮化疗前都会用类固醇。

577
00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:36.180
And I found that the steroids, the side effects of the steroids,
我发现类固醇的副作用，

578
00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:41.880
having me be real amped and then crashing kind of right as the chemo would hit me,
让我整个人极度亢奋，然后紧接着在化疗药效上来时又陷入低谷，

579
00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:44.180
that was a rough couple of days.
那几天真的很难熬。

580
00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.800
Yeah, that was what really, that was difficult for me.
没错，那确实让我感到非常痛苦。

581
00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:52.600
But with the breast cancer, that sort of came out of nowhere.
但乳腺癌的出现简直是突如其来。

582
00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:58.400
I was speaking at Sloan Kettering about being a cancer survivor with ovarian cancer
当时我正在斯隆-凯特琳癌症中心演讲，分享作为卵巢癌幸存者的经历，

583
00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:01.820
and then came home and discovered I had breast cancer, which was kind of ironic.
结果回家就发现自己得了乳腺癌，真是讽刺。

584
00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:07.200
And because it ran in my, runs in my family, I said, you know, just take both off
因为我有家族病史，所以我说，干脆把两边都切了吧，

585
00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:11.220
because I had, mainly it was in my left breast,
因为主要病灶是在左乳，

586
00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:13.980
but there were some troubling things in my right breaSt.
但右乳也有一些令人担忧的迹象。

587
00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:16.340
And so I decided to have them both removed.
所以我决定把两边都切除。

588
00:32:16.520 --> 00:32:18.980
I didn't opt for reconstruction.
我没有选择做重建手术。

589
00:32:19.180 --> 00:32:20.380
I don't know why.
我也不知道为什么。

590
00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:23.780
I think at the time I just, I didn't want to do it.
我想当时我就是不想做。

591
00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:34.100
And they put drains in and they're very thick, heavy, not heavy, but thick plastic drains.
他们给我装了引流管，那是很粗、很硬的塑料管，虽然不算重，但很粗。

592
00:32:34.100 --> 00:32:35.340
I'm familiar.
我了解。

593
00:32:35.340 --> 00:32:36.520
I had the drains.
我也用过引流管。

594
00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:36.980
Yes.
是的。

595
00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:37.520
Yeah.
没错。

596
00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:42.160
And for some reason on the left side, it must have hit a nerve.
不知为何，左侧的引流管一定是碰到了神经。

597
00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:44.680
I don't know what it was, but it was so painful.
我不知道具体是什么原因，但真的太疼了。

598
00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:49.380
And I realized when they removed it, that's what the extra pain was.
后来拔掉引流管时我才意识到，那额外的疼痛就是它引起的。

599
00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:51.840
There was the pain involved.
那是治疗过程中伴随的痛苦。

600
00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:55.980
And then I developed lymphedema after that, which my arms were very swollen.
后来我还出现了淋巴水肿，双臂肿胀得非常厉害。

601
00:32:56.260 --> 00:32:58.540
I could only wear men's shirts for a long time.
很长一段时间里，我只能穿男士衬衫。

602
00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:05.780
And through my doctor here, I met Bill Rapisi, who is the CEO of the Lymphatic Education and
通过我的医生，我认识了比尔·拉皮西（Bill Rapisi），他是淋巴教育与

603
00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:06.540
Research Network.
研究网络（Lymphatic Education and Research Network）的首席执行官。

604
00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:09.360
He asked me to come aboard as a national spokesperson.
他邀请我担任全国代言人。

605
00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:12.260
And I said, Bill, I don't, I've never done this before.
我对他说，比尔，我从来没做过这种事。

606
00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:15.380
I don't know if I can do a good job, what's involved.
我不知道自己能不能做好，也不知道具体要做些什么。

607
00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:22.120
But he told me a couple of really disturbing facts that nearly 10 million people in this
但他告诉我几个非常令人不安的事实：这个国家有近1000万人

608
00:33:22.120 --> 00:33:24.300
country suffer from some kind of lymphedema.
患有某种形式的淋巴水肿。

609
00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:28.360
And about 50,000 of those are congenital.
其中约5万人是先天性的。

610
00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:31.140
So it strikes kids.
所以它也会影响孩子。

611
00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:36.440
When we were lobbying in Congress, I met a man who lost three kids to pulmonary lymphedema.
我们在国会游说时，我遇到一位男士，他的三个孩子都死于肺淋巴水肿。

612
00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:44.380
Doctors in medical school, in four years of medical school, spend 15 minutes on learning
医学院的学生在四年的学习中，只有15分钟的时间

613
00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:45.920
about the lymphatic system.
用来学习淋巴系统。

614
00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:49.780
And see, Kathy, I did not know what lymphedema was.
你看，凯西，我以前也不知道什么是淋巴水肿。

615
00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:53.800
And when you started talking about it is when I learned about it.
直到你开始谈论它，我才有所了解。

616
00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:56.160
And Jenna had shared with me as well.
珍娜也曾跟我提起过。

617
00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:58.660
So I think this is so important to talk about.
所以我认为讨论这个话题非常重要。

618
00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:04.160
Yeah, something like 20% of breast cancer patients develop lymphedema.
没错，大约20%的乳腺癌患者会患上淋巴水肿。

619
00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:11.140
And it's because when you get your breast cancer surgery, something that is just standard is
这是因为在进行乳腺癌手术时，有一项标准程序，

620
00:34:11.140 --> 00:34:15.980
they remove some of your lymph nodes to test them for cancer to see if the cancer has spread
就是切除部分淋巴结进行癌细胞检测，看看癌症是否已经扩散。

621
00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:21.640
that from the breast, those lymph nodes underneath your arm, that's first stop shopping for the
从乳房扩散出来的癌细胞，腋下的淋巴结是

622
00:34:21.640 --> 00:34:22.020
cancer.
第一站。

623
00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:26.220
So they test those lymph nodes, but they remove them.
所以他们会检测这些淋巴结，但也会把它们切除。

624
00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:34.140
And when you mess with the lymphatic system like that, when you do that surgery, you increase
当你那样干扰淋巴系统，进行那种手术时，你患上

625
00:34:34.140 --> 00:34:36.200
your risk of developing lymphedema.
淋巴水肿的风险就会增加。

626
00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:43.000
And it was something that luckily my doctor did explain to me before my surgery.
幸运的是，我的医生在手术前向我解释过这一点。

627
00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:50.640
They educated me on the warning signs about my arms swelling or my legs or ankles swelling.
他们教导我注意手臂、腿部或脚踝肿胀的预警信号。

628
00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:53.820
And my cancer was on my left side.
我的癌症是在左侧。

629
00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.760
And I had my lymph nodes removed on my left side.
所以我的左侧淋巴结被切除了。

630
00:34:56.760 --> 00:35:01.860
And they have told me that whenever I get a blood pressure cuff, it needs to be on my right
他们告诉我，以后量血压时，必须用我的右

631
00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:02.300
side.
手臂。

632
00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:06.540
And if I'm going to get an IV, it needs to be placed on my right side.
如果需要打点滴，也必须打在我的右侧。

633
00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:13.700
Because that is just how incredibly vulnerable your lymphatic system is to disturbance.
因为淋巴系统对干扰极其脆弱。

634
00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:20.040
Well, I'm thrilled to hear you say all of that, because that means that the doctors have learned
听到你这么说我很高兴，因为这意味着医生们已经了解了

635
00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:23.040
about it and are making patients aware.
这一点，并开始提醒患者注意了。

636
00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:32.220
When I had breast cancer and I had my breast cancer done at a top-notch place here, but
我患乳腺癌时，是在这里一家顶尖的机构做的手术，但

637
00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:34.240
the doctors didn't talk to me about that.
医生并没有跟我谈过这些。

638
00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:41.040
I kept talking to them about it because I had been dating a guy at the time who had it in
我一直跟他们提起这件事，因为当时我在和一个患有此病的男人约会，

639
00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:45.580
his left arm and it's progressive and it's incurable.
他的左臂患病，而且这种病是进行性的，无法治愈。

640
00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:50.640
And he had gone through an experimental treatment to survive cancer and did not want to go to
他曾为了抗癌接受过实验性治疗，之后就再也不想

641
00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:54.120
another doctor until, you know, ever in his life.
去看医生了，你知道的，这辈子都不想了。

642
00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:57.000
And as a result, his arm became like wood.
结果，他的手臂变得像木头一样僵硬。

643
00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:58.100
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

644
00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:00.520
And that was my fear.
那正是我的恐惧所在。

645
00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:06.000
And also that, you know, I was afraid that that was what lymphedema was.
而且，你知道，我当时很害怕那就是淋巴水肿的样子。

646
00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:10.460
And I was full of rage because I had told the doctors, I'm worried about this.
我感到非常愤怒，因为我曾告诉医生，我很担心这个。

647
00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:11.300
I'm worried about this.
我很担心这个。

648
00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:12.060
I'm worried about this.
我很担心这个。

649
00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:13.700
And they took so many lymph nodes.
而且他们切除了太多的淋巴结。

650
00:36:14.100 --> 00:36:17.600
But I'm thrilled that they told you all of that.
但我很高兴他们告诉了你所有这些信息。

651
00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:22.440
Because back then, my doctor had to go and look it up on the internet.
因为那时候，我的医生还得去网上查。

652
00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:23.360
Wow.
哇。

653
00:36:23.860 --> 00:36:24.280
Yeah.
是啊。

654
00:36:24.480 --> 00:36:32.180
He found this woman, wonderful Dr. Emily Eicher, who was able to work with me and get my arms
他找到了这位了不起的艾米丽·艾彻医生，她能够配合我治疗，让我的手臂

655
00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:33.960
down over a period of time.
在一段时间内消肿了。

656
00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:37.700
And now that I've lost a lot of weight, that really helped.
现在我瘦了很多，这真的很有帮助。

657
00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:42.860
But trying to educate people over the last 10 years has been a real journey.
但在过去十年里，试图去教育大众真的是一段漫长的旅程。

658
00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:45.180
We would get emails from people.
我们会收到人们发来的邮件。

659
00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:51.400
I remember one guy, I'll never forget him, John Dale, who wrote us an email.
我记得有个人，我永远忘不了他，约翰·戴尔，他给我们写了一封邮件。

660
00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:57.720
And at the end of the email, all capital letters, help me, help me, help me, help me, help me.
在邮件的末尾，他用全大写字母写着：救救我，救救我，救救我，救救我，救救我。

661
00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:00.360
Because the people are in so much pain.
因为这些人正承受着巨大的痛苦。

662
00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:01.720
They don't know who to go to.
他们不知道该去找谁。

663
00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:05.260
I'm really proud of all of the work that we've done with LEARN.
我为我们通过LEARN所做的一切工作感到非常自豪。

664
00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:07.720
Our CEO has gone all around the world.
我们的首席执行官走遍了世界各地。

665
00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:13.480
We have now the first National Commission on Lymphatic Disease at the NIH.
我们现在在国立卫生研究院（NIH）设立了首个国家淋巴疾病委员会。

666
00:37:13.720 --> 00:37:16.360
They had their first meeting a couple of months ago.
几个月前他们召开了第一次会议。

667
00:37:16.700 --> 00:37:19.100
And we worked really hard to have that happen.
为了实现这一目标，我们付出了巨大的努力。

668
00:37:19.100 --> 00:37:26.220
And Rosa DeLauro of Connecticut, the congresswoman there, was instrumental in getting us the attention
康涅狄格州的女众议员罗莎·德劳罗在帮助我们获得关注方面发挥了关键作用，

669
00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:28.880
that we needed to get this commission going.
让我们得以推动这个委员会的成立。

670
00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:35.140
We got a grant from the CDC for publicity to really create our outreach for people.
我们从疾控中心（CDC）获得了一笔宣传拨款，真正开展了针对大众的推广工作。

671
00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:40.820
We're now working on the DOD because it's not just underneath our arMs.
我们现在正在与国防部（DOD）合作，因为这不仅仅是腋下的问题。

672
00:37:40.820 --> 00:37:49.180
If these veterans come back and their traumatic injuries have damaged their lymph system, they're at risk.
如果这些退伍军人回来时，他们的创伤性损伤损害了淋巴系统，他们就处于危险之中。

673
00:37:49.460 --> 00:37:52.180
We have a man who wanted to be in the Coast Guard.
我们有一位想加入海岸警卫队的人。

674
00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:56.080
And he came back from military and he was just a mess.
他从军队回来时，整个人状态非常糟糕。

675
00:37:56.300 --> 00:37:58.340
And he had to give up that dream.
他不得不放弃那个梦想。

676
00:37:58.340 --> 00:38:06.980
And that's been the most moving and sad parts of this is realizing that people had to give up their dreams because of...
这是最令人动容也最悲伤的部分，意识到人们不得不因为这些原因放弃梦想……

677
00:38:06.980 --> 00:38:12.220
And there's a kind of lymphedema that happens when you're like late teens, early 20s,
还有一种淋巴水肿，发生在十几岁到二十出头的时候，

678
00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:14.760
when you're just beginning your life and out of nowhere.
当你刚开始生活时，它就突然出现了。

679
00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:16.560
They don't know what it is.
他们不知道那是什么。

680
00:38:16.700 --> 00:38:19.340
It's some predisposition that occurs at that time.
那是当时发生的一种易感性病变。

681
00:38:19.340 --> 00:38:26.600
And I appreciate so much you're letting me talk about it on your show because education is key.
非常感谢你让我能在你的节目中谈论这个话题，因为教育是关键。

682
00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:33.120
And I always tell the people that they're at our lobby sessions in Washington, D.C.,
我总是告诉那些在华盛顿特区参加我们游说会议的人，

683
00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:34.880
your pain is your power.
你的痛苦就是你的力量。

684
00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:37.300
Your story is your power.
你的故事就是你的力量。

685
00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:44.980
And the fact that you can share your story with me and you can tell people how far we've come.
你能与我分享你的故事，并告诉大家我们已经取得了多大的进步，这本身就很有意义。

686
00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:46.280
Yeah.
是啊。

687
00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:53.220
It's amazing that you have perky breasts, you know?
你的胸部变得挺拔了，这太神奇了，你知道吗？

688
00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:57.300
I do like how my shirts fit me better now.
我确实喜欢我现在穿衬衫更合身的感觉。

689
00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:58.600
I'll say that.
我得说。

690
00:38:59.960 --> 00:39:03.760
And my hair, Kathy, after chemotherapy...
还有我的头发，凯西，化疗之后……

691
00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:08.020
So before chemotherapy, my hair was almost all gray.
化疗前，我的头发几乎全白了。

692
00:39:08.500 --> 00:39:13.600
And after chemotherapy, my hair is growing in not gray.
而化疗后，长出来的新头发不再是灰白色的了。

693
00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:14.660
Oh, my God.
天哪。

694
00:39:14.660 --> 00:39:18.540
So I heard that people said, oh, if you have straight hair, it might come back curly.
我听说有人说，哦，如果你原本是直发，长出来可能会变卷。

695
00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:21.200
If you have curly hair, like it'll come back a different texture.
如果你原本是卷发，长出来的质地会不一样。

696
00:39:21.360 --> 00:39:22.780
It might come back a different color.
颜色也可能会变。

697
00:39:23.140 --> 00:39:25.160
And you're not having to color your hair.
那你现在就不用染发了。

698
00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:25.780
No.
是的。

699
00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:27.140
How about that?
那不是很好吗？

700
00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:28.340
That's so great.
那真是太棒了。

701
00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:28.980
I'm jelly.
我好羡慕。

702
00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:30.260
I'm totally jelly.
我真是太羡慕了。

703
00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:33.500
No, I lost everything.
不，我失去了一切。

704
00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:35.720
I lost every hair everywhere.
我浑身上下的毛发都掉光了。

705
00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:37.200
Some of it I'm glad I loSt.
有些毛发掉了我反而挺高兴的。

706
00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:40.880
But it took me a while to get it back.
但我也花了好一阵子才让它们长回来。

707
00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:41.420
Yeah.
是啊。

708
00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:42.460
It was hard.
那段日子很难熬。

709
00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:43.500
They don't...
他们不……

710
00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:48.240
You know, when I heard that I was going to have to have chemo, my first two thoughts were,
你知道吗，当我听说自己必须接受化疗时，我头两个念头是，

711
00:39:48.420 --> 00:39:50.480
I don't want to throw up and I don't want to lose my hair.
我不想呕吐，也不想掉头发。

712
00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:53.460
That's what I associated chemotherapy with.
这就是我当时对化疗的联想。

713
00:39:53.460 --> 00:39:58.600
And I didn't throw up, but I did lose my hair.
我确实没吐，但我确实掉光了头发。

714
00:39:58.600 --> 00:40:03.800
And it didn't occur to me until it was happening that it is all your hair.
直到事情真正发生时，我才意识到掉的是全身的毛发。

715
00:40:04.280 --> 00:40:08.980
It's your eyebrows and your eyelashes and all of it everywhere.
包括眉毛、睫毛，浑身上下所有的毛发。

716
00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:10.480
And it's weird.
那种感觉很怪。

717
00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:13.420
My eyebrows came back real quickly, though.
不过我的眉毛长得挺快的。

718
00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:17.180
I was happy for that because I was not great at drawing them in.
我很庆幸，因为我不太擅长画眉毛。

719
00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:21.740
But it's really weird to not have eyelashes.
但没有睫毛的感觉真的很奇怪。

720
00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:22.940
Yeah.
是啊。

721
00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:23.100
You know?
你懂吗？

722
00:40:23.360 --> 00:40:23.620
Yeah.
懂。

723
00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:25.880
I don't think my eyelashes really came back.
我觉得我的睫毛好像没怎么长回来。

724
00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:27.800
Or maybe it's just being older.
又或者只是因为年纪大了。

725
00:40:28.420 --> 00:40:28.940
Yeah.
是啊。

726
00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:34.460
I had a friend of mine, actually, Judy Corey, who did my hair for Misery.
其实我有个朋友，朱迪·科里，她曾为《危情十日》帮我做过造型。

727
00:40:34.740 --> 00:40:37.660
She came and that was back in 2003.
那是2003年的事了，她过来了。

728
00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:40.540
She came over and shaved my head for me.
她过来帮我剃了光头。

729
00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:44.860
And then after I had a bald head, I mean, I did use all these...
剃光头之后，我是说，我确实用了所有这些……

730
00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:48.760
I had things made like caps and all this bullshit wigs and everything.
我让人做了些帽子，还有那些乱七八糟的假发之类的东西。

731
00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:49.900
And finally, I just thought...
最后，我心想……

732
00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:56.380
I saw Melissa Etheridge, you know, just wailing on her guitar on some kind of a show with a
我看到梅丽莎·埃瑟里奇，你知道的，在某个节目里光着头，

733
00:40:56.380 --> 00:40:56.780
bald head.
在那儿疯狂弹奏吉他。

734
00:40:56.820 --> 00:40:57.680
And I thought, oh, okay.
我就想，哦，好吧。

735
00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:02.460
You know, time to just stop all this hiding and just be who you are.
你知道，是时候停止躲藏，做回真实的自己了。

736
00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:06.280
But I'm thrilled that there are all of these new...
但我很高兴现在有了所有这些新的……

737
00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:07.920
What was the H one that you said?
你刚才说的那个H开头的是什么？

738
00:41:08.020 --> 00:41:08.340
Herpa?
赫帕？

739
00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.060
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

740
00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:10.240
Herceptin.
赫赛汀。

741
00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:11.500
Herceptin.
赫赛汀。

742
00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:11.980
Yeah.
对。

743
00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:13.660
It's a targeted immunotherapy.
这是一种靶向免疫疗法。

744
00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:17.460
I mean, really, it's only been out for about 25 years.
我是说，真的，它问世才大约25年。

745
00:41:17.920 --> 00:41:23.960
And it has just drastically changed the prognosis for women with HER2-positive breast cancers.
它彻底改变了HER2阳性乳腺癌女性患者的预后。

746
00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:24.780
Wow.
哇。

747
00:41:25.060 --> 00:41:26.180
It's really amazing.
这真的很了不起。

748
00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:27.360
Wow.
哇。

749
00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:28.580
Oh, bless you.
哦，上帝保佑你。

750
00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:29.800
You didn't have to go through that.
你不用经历那些痛苦。

751
00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:31.800
And you're not having any problems with your arm.
你的手臂现在没问题了吧。

752
00:41:32.380 --> 00:41:33.380
I am not.
没问题。

753
00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:33.940
No.
没有。

754
00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:36.260
And I'm really grateful for that.
对此我真的非常感激。

755
00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:41.820
And after my surgery, my doctor also prescribed some physical therapy.
手术后，医生还给我开了物理治疗。

756
00:41:41.960 --> 00:41:46.800
So I was able to get that side moving.
所以我能够让那一侧活动起来。

757
00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:49.020
You know, it's very tender, though.
不过，你知道，那里还是很敏感。

758
00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:56.520
I have to say, of all my scars, of all of the ways that I've been poked and prodded in this
我得说，在我所有的伤疤中，在我经历的各种检查和折腾中，

759
00:41:56.520 --> 00:42:04.840
process of getting a port and all the things, that scar underneath my arm from where they
包括植入输液港以及其他种种，我腋下切除淋巴结留下的那道伤疤

760
00:42:04.840 --> 00:42:07.160
remove the lymph nodes is the most tender.
是最敏感的。

761
00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:11.640
It's still tight when I try to raise my arm above my head.
当我试着把手臂举过头顶时，那里依然感觉很紧。

762
00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:15.120
It is really, truly such a sensitive area.
那确实是一个非常敏感的部位。

763
00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:17.380
Yeah, it's going to be tender, I think.
是啊，我想它会一直很敏感的。

764
00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:18.620
I mean, this is new.
毕竟，这是新伤。

765
00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:21.000
You've only had it, what, for a year, gone through it?
你才经历这些多久，一年吗？

766
00:42:21.460 --> 00:42:21.940
Yeah.
是的。

767
00:42:22.180 --> 00:42:25.060
January is the anniversary of my surgery.
一月份就是我手术的一周年纪念了。

768
00:42:26.100 --> 00:42:27.820
Look at all you've done in a year.
看看你这一年里做了多少事。

769
00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:28.740
I know.
我知道。

770
00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:35.560
Well, you know, Kathy, I remember you worked right after your chemotherapy.
嗯，凯西，我记得你在化疗后立刻就去工作了。

771
00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:37.820
You went back to work pretty quickly.
你很快就回到了工作岗位。

772
00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:43.260
And I read something that you said, which was that it was a little bit of an opportunity
我读过你的一段话，你说那是一个小小的机会，

773
00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:44.920
to be not a cancer patient.
让你暂时不用做一个癌症病人。

774
00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:46.380
You got to play a character.
你可以去扮演一个角色。

775
00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:49.900
And I have kept working through my treatment.
我在治疗期间也一直坚持工作。

776
00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:53.120
And I needed that for my mental health.
为了心理健康，我需要这样做。

777
00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:56.200
I needed a place that I went throughout the week.
我需要一个每周都能去的地方。

778
00:42:56.200 --> 00:42:58.280
And I just laughed with my best friend.
我和我最好的朋友一起大笑。

779
00:42:58.740 --> 00:43:03.340
And we talked about these great memories of our time on this show together.
我们聊起了在节目中一起度过的美好回忆。

780
00:43:04.100 --> 00:43:07.380
Because really, truly, it is so many appointments.
因为说真的，预约检查实在太多了。

781
00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:13.860
It is so much of my life and my time is spent with doctors and being a patient that I needed
我的生活和时间大部分都花在看医生和做病人上，所以我需要

782
00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:15.200
a place where I wasn't that.
一个不用扮演那种角色的地方。

783
00:43:15.800 --> 00:43:17.120
Yeah, that's true.
是的，确实如此。

784
00:43:17.480 --> 00:43:22.040
Lynn Redbrave, God rest her, she was going through fifth stage breast cancer and on the
琳·雷德格雷夫，愿上帝安息，她当时正处于乳腺癌晚期，

785
00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:22.780
downward slope.
情况在走下坡路。

786
00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:24.920
I wasn't done with chemo there, actually.
其实那时候我还没做完化疗。

787
00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:26.900
I just was about to start it.
我才刚要开始。

788
00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:30.040
And I was having gastro probleMs.
而且我当时还有肠胃问题。

789
00:43:30.380 --> 00:43:32.900
And she said, you've got to do this.
她对我说，你一定要去做这件事。

790
00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:36.000
You can be somebody else, you know, as you're saying, for the day.
就像你说的，你可以暂时成为别人，哪怕只有一天。

791
00:43:36.840 --> 00:43:38.260
And she was right.
她是正确的。

792
00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:45.500
You know, it gave me, although I was pretty cranky for a while, because I just wasn't
你知道，这给了我机会，虽然我有一阵子脾气很暴躁，因为我还没

793
00:43:45.500 --> 00:43:46.780
healed yet.
完全康复。

794
00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:49.500
You know, it was a little too soon.
你知道，还是有点太早了。

795
00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:52.620
But it was also, I couldn't turn down the payday.
但同时，我也无法拒绝那笔报酬。

796
00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:53.980
I really needed the payday.
我真的很需要那笔钱。

797
00:43:54.120 --> 00:43:55.740
Because I really dropped out.
因为我当时真的退出了。

798
00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:56.820
I was scared.
我很害怕。

799
00:43:57.060 --> 00:43:58.180
I was one of those people.
我就是那种人。

800
00:43:58.360 --> 00:43:59.760
And this was with the ovarian.
那还是在患卵巢癌的时候。

801
00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:05.000
And I was one of those people who stayed up at night, you know, and looked at the internet
我曾经也是那种会在深夜里辗转反侧，你知道的，盯着互联网看的人，

802
00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:07.240
to see if I could divine anything.
想看看能不能从中预知些什么。

803
00:44:08.380 --> 00:44:10.700
But knock wood, it's been 20 years.
但谢天谢地，这已经是20年前的事了。

804
00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:14.400
And I don't think I'm going to be having more problems with that.
我想我以后应该不会再有那方面的困扰了。

805
00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:16.240
How are you doing emotionally?
你现在情绪状态怎么样？

806
00:44:17.300 --> 00:44:19.320
Oh, you're so kind to ask.
噢，你真是太贴心了，居然会问这个。

807
00:44:24.510 --> 00:44:26.250
How am I doing emotionally?
我情绪状态怎么样？

808
00:44:28.540 --> 00:44:30.540
I'm okay most of the time.
大部分时间我还可以。

809
00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:42.170
But at least once a week, I get mad because every day I feel like I have to swim upstream
但至少每周我都会有那么一次感到愤怒，因为我觉得每天我都得逆流而上，

810
00:44:42.170 --> 00:44:42.830
a little bit.
稍微挣扎一下。

811
00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:47.110
I have to swim against the current just to have a normal day.
我必须顶着阻力前行，才能过上正常的一天。

812
00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:48.850
And it's exhausting.
这真的让人精疲力竭。

813
00:44:50.350 --> 00:44:58.930
But I am really so very well supported by my husband and by friends, by Angela.
不过，我的丈夫、朋友们，还有安吉拉，确实给了我非常大的支持。

814
00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:06.850
But I did notice that there was a real swell of support during chemotherapy.
但我确实注意到，在化疗期间，支持的声音特别高涨。

815
00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:11.770
I think because people know how hard that is to get through.
我想是因为大家都知道那段过程有多难熬。

816
00:45:12.530 --> 00:45:16.790
And when the chemo was over, some of the support went away.
而当化疗结束后，一些支持就随之减少了。

817
00:45:17.630 --> 00:45:26.390
Sometimes I feel like I'm bothering people or I feel like they're going to get frustrated
有时我觉得自己是在打扰别人，或者觉得他们会对我感到厌烦，

818
00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:26.890
with me.
对我失去耐心。

819
00:45:27.030 --> 00:45:30.370
They're going to be like, geez, is this ever going to end with her?
他们可能会想，天哪，她这事儿到底什么时候才是个头啊？

820
00:45:31.450 --> 00:45:32.890
And that's how I feel.
我自己也是这种感觉。

821
00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:35.590
I feel like, geez, is this ever going to end with me?
我也会想，天哪，我这事儿到底什么时候才是个头啊？

822
00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:37.950
But I do still need support.
但我确实仍然需要支持。

823
00:45:37.950 --> 00:45:41.770
And I think that's something I would want people to know if they have a friend or a family
我想如果有人有朋友或家人正在经历这些，我希望他们能明白一点，那就是当所谓的治疗，

824
00:45:41.770 --> 00:45:47.950
member going through this, is that the struggle doesn't end when the treatments, quote unquote,
结束时，抗争并没有真正结束。

825
00:45:48.130 --> 00:45:48.450
end.
你懂吗？

826
00:45:48.870 --> 00:45:49.590
You know?
你说得太对了。

827
00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:51.290
I think that's a great point.
我想这正是我当初患卵巢癌时的亲身经历。

828
00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:54.870
And I think that's exactly what I experienced when I had ovarian cancer.
当时我有一位朋友非常支持我。

829
00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:58.890
You know, I had a friend at the time who was so supportive.
我记得有一次，我们正准备出门。

830
00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:04.510
And I remember at one point, we were getting ready to go somewhere.
我坐在台阶上系鞋带。

831
00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:07.950
And I was sitting on the step, lacing my shoes.
我的帽子掉了下来。

832
00:46:07.950 --> 00:46:09.790
And my cap fell off.
他笑了笑。

833
00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:11.070
And he sort of laughed.
我当时就说，这对现在的我来说太难了。

834
00:46:11.530 --> 00:46:14.330
And I just said, this is so hard for me.
后来我发现，随着我化疗的推进——那是针对卵巢癌的治疗——

835
00:46:14.810 --> 00:46:20.490
And then I found out that as I progressed through the chemotherapy, this was for ovarian,
人们确实会有那样的反应。

836
00:46:21.950 --> 00:46:24.870
that people have exactly that reaction.
只不过他们离开得更早，因为我的治疗持续了九个月。

837
00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:29.310
Only they left sooner during my, because it went on for nine months.
我的医生本来建议我做六个月，但他看我状态不错，

838
00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:33.010
My doctor wanted me to do it for six months, but he said, you're doing well.
就说，那我们再多做三个月吧。

839
00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:35.030
So let's do it, you know, another three months.
结果人们就渐渐疏远了，因为他们会说，哇，这还没完吗？

840
00:46:35.910 --> 00:46:41.890
And people just sort of dropped away because they didn't, they said, wow, you know, isn't
这还没结束吗？

841
00:46:41.890 --> 00:46:42.710
this over?
他们确实没有那种耐心。

842
00:46:42.930 --> 00:46:43.790
Isn't it going to be done?
所以，后来我都是独自去参加化疗的。

843
00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:46.750
Is it, you know, they didn't have the, the patience really.
我最后干脆就自己一个人去了。

844
00:46:48.070 --> 00:46:53.770
And, um, so I went to my chemotherapy sessions by myself.
自己去反而更轻松，还能期待一下能不能碰上那个长得很帅的俄罗斯护士，

845
00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:58.310
I got to the point of where I would just go by myself.
他会走进来。

846
00:46:58.310 --> 00:47:03.610
It was easier to go and hope that I got the really good looking Russian nurse who would
我不记得他叫什么名字了。

847
00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:04.090
come in.
想不起来了，是瓦西里还是别的什么，或者是伊万之类的。

848
00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:06.310
I don't know what his name was.
我当时就想，天哪，他今天居然在。

849
00:47:06.430 --> 00:47:09.430
I can't remember, Vasily or something like that, or Yvonne or what.
我当时就想，天哪，他今天居然在。

850
00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:11.710
I was like, oh my God, he's here today.
我当时就想，天哪，他今天居然在。

851
00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:12.290
Great, great.
太好了，太好了。

852
00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:16.590
But it would take about four hours, you know, to be in there.
但你知道，在那儿待着得花上四个小时左右。

853
00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:18.730
And they gave me a private room and all of that.
他们还给了我一间私人病房，诸如此类的待遇。

854
00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:23.950
So being a VIP help and thank God with the breast cancer, I didn't have to go through
所以作为贵宾确实有帮助，感谢上帝，因为患的是乳腺癌，我没必要经历

855
00:47:23.950 --> 00:47:26.150
chemotherapy or radiation.
化疗或放疗。

856
00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:29.830
And which is one reason why I opted to just make it a double.
这也是我选择直接做双侧切除手术的原因之一。

857
00:47:31.730 --> 00:47:32.250
Yeah.
是的。

858
00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:37.810
But I was really enraged after, but we talked about that before.
但我当时确实非常愤怒，不过我们之前谈过这个了。

859
00:47:37.810 --> 00:47:43.470
And, and, and it wasn't until I sat with Dr. Eicher, you know, and I told her all of the
直到我坐在艾彻医生（Dr. Eicher）对面，你知道，我把所有

860
00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:45.730
things I was feeling and the rage that I was feeling.
我的感受以及我感到的那种愤怒都告诉了她。

861
00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:52.610
And she's this charming little woman, Czechoslovakian, and she's just said something like, okay,
她是一位迷人的小个子女士，捷克裔，她只是说了些类似这样的话：好吧，

862
00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:58.150
well, let's have a glass of champagne and then we will begin the rest of your life.
来喝杯香槟，然后我们就开启你余下的人生吧。

863
00:48:02.570 --> 00:48:08.190
So somehow she just slowly pulled me out of my rage and then working with, with learn,
所以不知怎么的，她慢慢地把我从愤怒中拉了出来，然后通过学习，

864
00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:16.570
I was able to use it, you know, to, to give it purpose, to give it purpose, to give back,
我能够利用它，你知道，赋予它意义，赋予它意义，去回馈社会，

865
00:48:16.730 --> 00:48:20.330
to do something real in the real world, which is like what you guys are doing.
在现实世界中做些实实在在的事情，就像你们正在做的那样。

866
00:48:20.870 --> 00:48:24.210
Angela, have you ever had to deal with anything like this?
安吉拉，你曾经不得不处理过类似的事情吗？

867
00:48:24.830 --> 00:48:25.030
Yes.
是的。

868
00:48:25.250 --> 00:48:30.150
My grandmother and my aunt were all breast cancer survivors.
我的祖母和阿姨都是乳腺癌幸存者。

869
00:48:30.450 --> 00:48:32.730
My grandmother passed away, but not from cancer.
我祖母去世了，但不是因为癌症。

870
00:48:33.170 --> 00:48:39.210
And my, my aunt, my mom's sister had a really rough go, but she's doing much better now.
而我妈妈的姐妹，也就是我阿姨，当时情况非常糟糕，但她现在好多了。

871
00:48:39.370 --> 00:48:44.250
So I go and, you know, get my mammograms and try to stay on top of it.
所以我定期去进行乳房X光检查，尽量密切关注自己的健康。

872
00:48:44.590 --> 00:48:50.310
But, you know, when Jenna got her diagnosis, all I wanted to do was be a sorcerer.
但是，你知道，当珍娜确诊时，我唯一想做的就是成为一名巫师。

873
00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:55.070
And I wanted to just be strong for her.
我只想为她变得坚强。

874
00:48:55.410 --> 00:49:02.510
And, you know, we never really talked it out about just the, the scary side of it and the,
而且，你知道，我们从未真正谈论过这件事可怕的一面，以及

875
00:49:02.510 --> 00:49:07.090
those emotions until we sat down on our podcast to talk about it for the first time.
那些情绪，直到我们在播客上第一次坐下来谈论它。

876
00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:13.910
And I think I cried for 20 minutes and we couldn't even use it because it was the first time I said
我想我哭了20分钟，我们甚至没法播出那段录音，因为那是我第一次说出

877
00:49:13.910 --> 00:49:16.730
out loud, she's cancer free.
她已经战胜癌症了。

878
00:49:16.730 --> 00:49:19.730
